WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.130 --> 00:06.020
You have about two minutes to leave an impact on your audience. After that, you start to lose their attention.

2
00:06.470 --> 00:12.000
So in those two minutes, you really need to convey your most important messages up front.

3
00:12.360 --> 00:17.740
Don’t spend that time giving all your evidence and supporting arguments to get to your main point.

4
00:17.740 --> 00:23.230
Start with your main point, and then layer in your supporting arguments and your evidence behind it.

5
00:23.940 --> 00:27.300
We call this the pyramid principle. So if you think of a pyramid,

6
00:27.620 --> 00:36.771
your most important point is up top, and that’s where you start. And then you layer in the supporting arguments and all the myriad of facts that underlie those, down at the bottom.

7
00:36.771 --> 00:41.600
So let’s talk about what’s in the pyramid. At the top of the pyramid is your governing thought.

8
00:41.730 --> 00:48.196
This is the key takeaway that you want people to hear. This is the thing that you communicate in the first two minutes.

9
00:48.196 --> 00:55.640
The governing thought is essentially the answer to the question that you’re trying to answer, right? So if you’ve gone into a meeting and

10
00:55.810 --> 01:10.840
either the CFO or the CEO or your manager has asked you to go away and think about: should we invest in the following technology? Should we expand into the following regions? How should we improve our sales force productivity?

11
01:11.010 --> 01:14.690
Beneath that is another layer, which are your key line statements.

12
01:15.140 --> 01:19.710
No more than three to five. If you have more than that, people start to get lost.

13
01:20.170 --> 01:22.720
That’s really all people can handle or remember:

14
01:23.300 --> 01:27.680
three to five key line statements that really compose your supporting argument.

15
01:28.010 --> 01:33.410
And these are the key statements, essentially, that answer the question why or how.

16
01:33.930 --> 01:41.730
Right, so you’ve made a governing thought: yes, we should invest in the following technology, because it will improve our productivity by 20%.

17
01:42.090 --> 01:49.060
We should expand into the following geography because it’s a growing market and fits within our portfolio. The bottom layer

18
01:49.670 --> 02:02.268
is comprised of your foundational facts. These are all the facts, all the pieces of information, that actually synthesize up into your key line statements, that then boil up into your governing thought.

19
02:02.268 --> 02:07.070
An example: where you’re driving towards a large-scale organizational change,

20
02:07.300 --> 02:15.680
the governing thought at the top of your pyramid would be what you’re trying to create. What are you trying to build as a result of this change journey you’re embarking on?

21
02:16.010 --> 02:21.044
The supporting arguments underneath would be the themes of what you’re doing to get there.

22
02:21.044 --> 02:28.930
And then the foundational arguments at the bottom would be lists of initiatives and specific actions that you’re taking to drive that change.

